item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated testing is neither documented nor explicitly excluded."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "70% in one reported run",
          "reason": "One reported score supplies no evidence of consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.3 and 9.7 are reinterpreted as consistency across repeated AI evaluations. Repeat-run precision is unrateable from the supplied evidence (0), not demonstrated inadequate. Sections 3 and 10 distinguish exact-grid scoring from evidence supporting score interpretations."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The developers explicitly exclude repeated testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "ran the evaluation exactly once",
          "reason": "A single evaluation cannot establish repeat-run stability; it also does not demonstrate instability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA 9.3, 9.7 and 9.8, reinterpreted for repeated AI evaluations, repeat-run precision remains unsupported (0). Explicit absence of repetitions contradicts the procedural claim without establishing poor score consistency."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations under matching conditions are explicitly reported."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's stability definition of at most two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.7 accommodates this repeat-run range as alternative precision evidence, with 9.3 informing consistency assessment. The evidence supports the stated local stability criterion. Ten repetitions are not ten independent systems, and the range alone does not justify a general reliability coefficient or an excellent EFPA rating."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings",
          "reason": "The developers explicitly examined repeated evaluations under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The observed variation exceeds the stated stability limit of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 9.7 and 9.8 distinguish conducting a precision study from obtaining satisfactory precision. The findings demonstrate inadequate stability for the report's stated criterion, rather than missing evidence. This conclusion is confined to the tested system and conditions."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance does not document repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The new instructions supply no evidence of score consistency."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.3.8 addresses software instructions; their addition does not supply reliability evidence under 9.3 or 9.7. Repeat-run precision remains unrateable (0). Documentation quality and demonstrated measurement quality require separate judgments."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "A achieved the higher score on the evaluated puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The results do not establish comparative general intelligence across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 2.2, 3 and 10.1–10.5 tie interpretation to intended use, content and validation evidence. The observed comparison fits the narrow intended use. General intelligence would require a broader validation argument. Section 8.3.2.2 permits a norms exception for an explicitly ranking-only purpose, without permitting broader generalization."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60",
          "reason": "The reported scores establish A's better performance on those puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Stating a broad intended use does not provide evidence supporting it."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 require evidence appropriate to the claimed interpretation; broader applications require broader validity support. The grid comparison is supported, but selecting for general intelligence across domains is not justified by the supplied content or findings. Missing broader evidence does not demonstrate that A lacks greater general intelligence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "Test scores alone do not establish comparative improvement in actual workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomized trial supports better correctness with A in the studied workshop; the reported difference interval is entirely positive."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Decision correctness findings do not establish an improvement in the unmeasured safety outcome."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA 10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2 address the randomized comparison and relevant external correctness criterion. Preregistration, random allocation, blinded checking and reported uncertainty strengthen support for the workshop-specific intended use. The 120 inspectors are the trial sample, not an established benchmark reliability sample. Criterion reliability and broader generalization remain insufficiently documented; safety benefits remain unestablished."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task clarification was needed. The original EFPA framework was retained, with only relevant sections discussed as permitted. AI systems replace human test takers where explicitly reinterpreted; human bodily prerequisites, clinical qualifications and personal debriefing are inapplicable to the AI itself. Human-facing requirements remain applicable where humans participate, as in C8. Unreported norms, materials, fairness studies and generated reports were not presumed absent or inadequate. Labels assess the exact statements, not EFPA ratings. No aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied."
}
